# rule.require.named-transformers

## .what
extract decode-friction into named transformers. the test: "do i have to decode this to understand what it produces?" yes → extract; no → inline
decode-friction = logic that needs mental simulation: positional access `slug.split('.')[0]!`, reduce, filter/map/sort pipelines, compound booleans, regex extracts, date math
not decode-friction: property access, named calls, simple conditionals, `arr.length`

## .heuristic
not named by us or an ehmpathy package? wrap it in a domain-named operation

## .examples
- `slug.split('.')[0]!` → `asKeyrackKeyOrg({ slug })`
- `items.reduce((s, i) => s + i.amount, 0)` → `computeTotal({ items })`
- `a && b || c && !d` → `isEligibleForDiscount({ order })`
- `await stripe.customers.create(...)` → `sdkStripe.setCustomer(...)`

names follow rule.require.get-set-gen-verbs (as*, is*, get*, compute*)

## .enforcement
decode-friction inline in an orchestrator = blocker (rule.forbid.inline-decode-friction)
